If you're wondering why both and not just MPL-2.0
──────────────────────────────────────────────────
Good question. Brave Browser is MPL-2.0, so offering MPL-2.0 here makes the
packaging code license-compatible with the software it packages. That part is
straightforward.

But MPL-2.0 alone has a limitation: it is a weak copyleft license. It requires
you to share modifications to MPL-licensed files, but it does not require the
larger work to be open source. Someone could take this flake, modify it, fold
it into a proprietary product, and never share those changes as long as they
keep the Nix files technically available.

GPL-3.0 closes that gap. It is a strong copyleft license — if you distribute
something that includes GPL-3.0 code, the whole thing must be GPL-3.0. That
means modifications cannot quietly disappear into a closed product.

By offering both, anyone in the free software / NixOS ecosystem can use this
under GPL-3.0 with its stronger guarantees, while anyone building something
that is already MPL-2.0 (or just needs the weaker copyleft) can use it under
that instead. No license mismatch, no friction, maximum compatibility.

If you just want to use the flake, none of this matters — do whatever you want.
If you are redistributing or building on top of it, pick the license that fits.
